<article class="env-lemma" id="08N4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08N4">Lemma <span data-tag="08N4">12.5.13</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ w\ar[r]^ f\ar[d]_ g &  y\ar[d]^ h\\  x\ar[r]^ k &  z }  \]
</div>
  <p> be a commutative diagram. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If the diagram is cartesian and $k$ is an epimorphism, then the diagram is cocartesian and $f$ is an epimorphism. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If the diagram is cocartesian and $g$ is a monomorphism, then the diagram is cartesian and $h$ is a monomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose the diagram is cartesian and $k$ is an epimorphism. Let $u = (g, f) : w \to x \oplus y$ and let $v = (k, -h) : x \oplus y \to z$. As $k$ is an epimorphism, $v$ is an epimorphism, too. Therefore and by Lemma <a href="/tag/08N2" data-tag="08N2">12.5.11</a>, the sequence $0\to w\overset {u}\to x\oplus y\overset {v}\to z\to 0$ is exact. Thus, the diagram is cocartesian by Lemma <a href="/tag/08N2" data-tag="08N2">12.5.11</a>. Finally, $f$ is an epimorphism by Lemma <a href="/tag/08N3" data-tag="08N3">12.5.12</a> and Lemma <a href="/tag/010C" data-tag="010C">12.5.4</a>. This proves (1), and (2) follows by duality. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>